1
00:00:03,380 --> 00:00:06,180
Let me show you how I cook a pheasant.

2
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:12,380
Now the way I don't cook a pheasant is to take a whole pheasant like this and throw it

3
00:00:12,380 --> 00:00:16,920
on a grill or whatever because the breasts are very, very lean.

4
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:18,820
They're going to cook very quickly.

5
00:00:19,140 --> 00:00:23,440
The legs and thighs are very sinewy, so they need to be cooked low and slow.

6
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:29,700
So I always start with a pheasant breakdown, removing all parts.

7
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:33,240
There's a leg.

8
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:45,240
There's a leg and I'm just going to cut straight down the breast bone to remove the breast.

9
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:47,480
This is a wild bird.

10
00:00:47,780 --> 00:00:49,560
You can tell there's not a bunch of fat on it.

11
00:00:50,620 --> 00:00:51,640
There's the breast.

12
00:00:54,580 --> 00:01:02,340
So these are much leaner than the pen-raised birds and certainly leaner than a chicken.

13
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:06,040
You just don't see any fat on here.

14
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:10,540
Now this carcass here, this is stock material.

15
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:16,680
So I save my pheasant pieces, pheasant parts to make stock at them.

16
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:22,480
Brown it with some celery carrot, onion, throw it into a stock pot, cold water, let it simmer.

17
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,960
It's so much better than anything you can buy out of a can or use with a bouillon cube.

18
00:01:28,340 --> 00:01:32,760
So even if you don't have a lot of pheasants, let's say you've got a couple of pheasants,

19
00:01:33,140 --> 00:01:37,680
you got a couple of chukar bodies, you've got a wild turkey that you've broken down.

20
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:41,600
You can break the whole thing down and make a delicious stock out of it.

21
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:47,880
But really cooking that pheasant all by itself, all together, doesn't make sense.

22
00:01:48,340 --> 00:01:53,580
So the legs and thighs, I'm going to brown them and then braise them.

23
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:58,760
And by braising, I'm going to throw that into a roasting pan, again with celery carrot,

24
00:01:58,940 --> 00:02:02,380
onion, a little bit of liquid, about three quarters of an inch of liquid.

25
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:06,740
Cover it up with foil or a tight fitting lid and it's going to take several hours

26
00:02:06,740 --> 00:02:11,260
at about 300 degrees before that pheasant meat pulls off the bone.

27
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:16,340
Then you can take the bones and add that to your stock pot and you're using the whole deal.

28
00:02:16,980 --> 00:02:19,660
But for this breast, very, very simple.

29
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:24,340
I've got some Lodge olive oil into a hot cast iron skillet.

30
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:27,500
I'm also going to put a little butter in there.

31
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:34,960
Once that skillet is hot and not a minute before I'm going to add the pheasant breast

32
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:42,500
and look again just at how thin these are and compare these to chicken or to a pen raised bird.

33
00:02:42,940 --> 00:02:45,920
The wild birds are a lot leaner but they taste great.

34
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:54,240
People that tell me that they think pheasant is gamey, I don't get it.

35
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:57,500
There's nothing gamey about a pheasant, a chukar, a quail.

36
00:02:58,300 --> 00:03:01,700
Here's one of the things that I've done with people that say that game is gamey.

37
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:05,160
I said, all right now this is elk.

38
00:03:05,700 --> 00:03:08,600
I know you don't like game but try this elk.

39
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:12,480
I don't think it's gamey at all and they'll try it and I'll go,

40
00:03:13,180 --> 00:03:19,440
you know it's still a little gamey and what I've done is I've given them a piece of beef instead

41
00:03:20,140 --> 00:03:21,700
and told them it was elk.

42
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:26,520
But just when I planted that seed they're thinking, oh yeah, it's gamey all right.

43
00:03:26,520 --> 00:03:28,040
Skin side down.

44
00:03:29,940 --> 00:03:32,500
I've got a little of the high mountain poultry rub.

45
00:03:34,320 --> 00:03:36,820
What's great about the high mountain products is that

46
00:03:38,220 --> 00:03:40,000
they're low in salt.

47
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:42,700
They're high in flavor, low in salt.

48
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:44,720
Let me flip this guy over.

49
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:48,360
I'm going to turn the heat down just a little bit.

50
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:51,860
And it's okay to flip it a time or two.

51
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:55,200
I'm going to season the beef side now with some more of the high mountain.

52
00:03:56,520 --> 00:03:57,300
Poultry rub.

53
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:03,140
Also a good hearty herb from the backyard.

54
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:04,820
I've got some rosemary.

55
00:04:05,340 --> 00:04:12,840
You just take the rosemary and you just strip it down the stem, the stalk, chop it up.

56
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:17,340
And you want to add the rosemary early on so that it releases the oils.

57
00:04:18,020 --> 00:04:19,160
It's good hearty flavor.

58
00:04:19,380 --> 00:04:22,540
It's not like basil cilantro parsley which is more delicate.

59
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:28,100
I've also got some garlic which I'm going to add right towards the end.

60
00:04:33,320 --> 00:04:37,200
And a little jalapeno for heat.

61
00:04:38,900 --> 00:04:41,480
Maybe a little more jalapeno for heat.

62
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:46,940
It's really close.

63
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:50,260
I mean real time this is super close.

64
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:54,020
I know that my pheasant is done because it's still going to be just

65
00:04:54,020 --> 00:04:56,040
a little tiny bit pink in the center.

66
00:04:56,480 --> 00:04:57,880
Nothing bad's going to happen.

